# Christie Korus Series Projector Driver
#
# Controls the Christie Korus Series 1DLP laser projectors (4K1000-KS,
# 4K1000A-KS, 4K1400-KS, 4K1400A-KS) over TCP 3002 or the RS232 IN port,
# using the serial commands in Christie's "Korus Series Serial Commands"
# Technical Reference 020-104044-01 Rev. 1 (01-2026). Messages are ASCII,
# enclosed in parentheses, with no line terminator:
#   SET     (CODE value)          e.g. (PWR 1), (SIN 3)
#   SET     (CODE+SUBC value)     e.g. (TPG+ITPG 5)
#   REQUEST (CODE?)               e.g. (PWR?)
#   REPLY   (CODE!value)          the reference's example: (SHU!0); its
#                                 format table also shows a number followed
#                                 by a quoted label: (PWR!001 "On")
#   ERROR   (65535 00000 ERR00005 "ITP: Too Few Parameters")
#
# Why its own driver and not part of christie_serial: the Korus reference
# reuses the M Series framing and many of its codes, but not their meanings.
# BRT and CON run 0 to 100 here (M Series -1000 to 1000), LPM 0 is Constant
# Power (M Series: Maximum Brightness), SOR is rear projection on or off
# (M Series: four orientations), the test patterns are numbered differently,
# inputs are one digit (3 = HDMI1, not a slot+input pair), focus, zoom and
# lens shift only step, and FTB, LPI, PJH and the SST subcodes are absent.
# One file cannot carry both sets of ranges and meanings.
#
# Format: YAML. A text protocol, request and reply, nothing a declarative
# driver cannot express.
#
# Framing: replies are read up to the closing parenthesis, which the
# reference gives as the end of every message, so a reply is read whether or
# not the projector sends a line ending after it. Every rule tolerates both
# reply forms the reference shows (a bare value, or a number and a quoted
# label), and leading zeros.
#
# Push vs poll: POLL-ONLY. The reference defines request and reply only: no
# subscription, notification or event mechanism. (The projector can send
# SNMP traps and email for its own alerts, per the Korus user manual, but no
# MIB is published.) The driver polls power and shutter every cycle, and the
# active source, signal, OSD, white peaking, warp and blend settings and
# light source hours while the projector is on.
#
# What is polled, and why only that: the reference lists a request form for
# PWR, SHU, OSD, WPK, WAP, BLD, MSV and the read-only codes (MIF, FVI, ILI,
# LET, SIV, SST and others). Brightness, contrast, input, lamp mode, picture
# mode and the rest list a set form only, so they are commands without a
# read-back. A sets-only command is not assumed to answer: the reference shows
# no reply to a set, so the driver reads the values back on the next poll.
#
# Unverified (no Korus on the bench): the reply form of every read-only
# query (the reference prints none), whether a set draws a reply, whether
# the active source is reported as a number, a name or both, and what the
# projector sends after the closing parenthesis. The simulator answers in
# the forms the reference's format table shows, and its read-only replies
# are placeholders.
#
# Standby: the Korus default standby mode is Communication mode, which keeps
# the network up and answers (PWR?) with 0. ECO mode drops the network in
# standby (Korus user manual 020-002159-03, Setting the standby mode), and the
# reference documents no network wake, so OpenAVC cannot turn on a projector
# left in ECO standby over the network.
#
# Protocol reference: Christie Korus Series Serial Commands, Technical
# Reference 020-104044-01 Rev. 1 (01-2026).

id: christie_korus
name: Christie Korus Series Projector
manufacturer: Christie
category: projector
version: 1.0.0
author: OpenAVC
description: >-
  Controls Christie Korus Series 1DLP laser projectors (4K1000-KS,
  4K1000A-KS, 4K1400-KS, 4K1400A-KS) over the network (TCP 3002) or
  RS-232. Power, shutter, input select, picture mode, brightness, contrast,
  color, sharpness, color temperature, gamma, image size, freeze, test
  patterns, light source mode and power, lens focus, zoom, shift and
  memories, OSD, warp and blend selection, standby mode and remote key
  emulation. Polls power, shutter, the active source and signal, and
  light source information.

source_url: https://www.christiedigital.com/globalassets/resources/public/020-104044-01-christie-lit-tech-ref-korus-series-api.pdf
tags: [projector, christie, korus, laser, 4k, serial-api, rs232, lan-control]
verified: false
simulated: true
ports: [3002]
min_platform_version: "0.37.0"
compatible_models:
  - manufacturer: Christie
    models:
      - 4K1000-KS
      - 4K1000A-KS
      - 4K1400-KS
      - 4K1400A-KS
    confidence: untested
    notes: >-
      Leave Standby Mode at Communication mode (the default) to
      turn the projector on over the network. Christie M Series, J, HS, H,
      GS, G, E and Roadie projectors use the Christie Projector driver.

transport: tcp
transports: [tcp, serial]
# The closing parenthesis ends every message (020-104044-01, Message
# structure); any line ending after it is left as leading whitespace on the
# next reply, which every rule allows.
delimiter: ")"

help:
  overview: >-
    Christie Korus Series laser projectors (4K1000-KS, 4K1000A-KS,
    4K1400-KS, 4K1400A-KS). Power, shutter, inputs (HDMI 1, HDMI 2,
    DisplayPort, 12G-SDI, HDBaseT), picture controls, test patterns, light
    source, lens, OSD, warp and blend selection. Power and shutter are read
    every 15 seconds; the active source, signal and light source hours are
    read while the projector is on. Brightness, contrast, input and the
    other picture controls are sent but not read back. For a command not
    listed here, use Send Raw Command with the Korus Series Serial Commands
    reference.
  setup: >-
    1. Network: connect the projector's RJ-45 port (or its HDBaseT port) and
       note its IP address (Menu > Configuration > Communications > LAN).
       Control uses TCP port 3002. No login.
    2. To turn the projector on from OpenAVC, leave Menu > Configuration >
       Power Management > Standby Mode at Communication mode, the default.
       In ECO mode the projector leaves the network while it is off.
    3. RS-232: connect the RS232 IN port with a null-modem cable (pins 2-3,
       3-2 and 5-5). The default is 115200 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1
       stop bit; match Baud Rate to Menu > Configuration > Communications >
       Serial Port Baud rate.
  connection: >-
    A projector in ECO standby does not answer on the network until it is
    turned on at the projector or with its remote. If the projector stops
    answering for about a minute it shows offline, and comes back as soon
    as it answers again.

discovery:
  # Active fingerprint: (SIV?), the serial command version. The Korus
  # reference documents it and the M Series reference (020-100224-11) has no
  # SIV code, so an M Series projector answers it with an error and is left
  # to christie_serial's (PWR?) probe. A Korus answers both probes; this
  # driver's id sorts first, so a scan pre-selects it and offers
  # christie_serial as the alternative. The reply form is the reference's
  # generic (Code!data); no SIV reply is printed in it.
  # OUI 00:1a:d7 is Christie Digital Systems' IEEE block, as in
  # christie_serial; a Korus built around another maker's network module
  # would carry that maker's prefix instead.
  tcp_probe:
    port: 3002
    send_ascii: "(SIV?)"
    expect_regex: '\(SIV!'
    extract_manufacturer: "Christie"
  oui:
    - "00:1a:d7"
  port_open:
    - 3002
  manufacturer_alias:
    - "christie"
    - "christie digital"

default_config:
  host: ""
  port: 3002
  poll_interval: 15
  inter_command_delay: 0.05
  baudrate: 115200
  bytesize: 8
  parity: "N"
  stopbits: 1

config_schema:
  host:
    type: string
    required: true
    label: IP Address
    description: The projector's IP address (Menu > Configuration > Communications > LAN).
  port:
    type: integer
    default: 3002
    label: TCP Port
    description: 3002 for the Korus serial commands over the network.
  poll_interval:
    type: integer
    default: 15
    min: 5
    label: Poll Interval (sec)
  inter_command_delay:
    type: number
    default: 0.05
    min: 0
    label: Inter-Command Delay (sec)
    description: Gap between commands. Raise it if the projector misses commands.
  baudrate:
    type: integer
    default: 115200
    label: Baud Rate (RS-232)
    description: 115200 is the Korus default. Match the projector's Serial Port Baud rate setting.

state_variables:
  power:
    type: enum
    values: ["off", "on", warming, cooling]
    label: Power
    control: true
    help: "From (PWR?): off = standby, on = the light source is on, warming = warming up, cooling = cooling down."
  shutter:
    type: enum
    values: [open, closed]
    label: Shutter
    control: true
    help: "From (SHU?): open = the picture is showing, closed = the shutter is blanking it."
  active_source:
    type: string
    label: Active Source
    help: "The active source as the projector reports it (MIF+ACTS?), read while the projector is on."
  signal_resolution:
    type: string
    label: Signal Resolution
    help: "The resolution of the active source as the projector reports it (MIF+RESL?), read while the projector is on."
  osd:
    type: boolean
    label: On-Screen Display
    control: true
    help: "From (OSD?): the projector's on-screen display is showing (true) or hidden (false)."
  white_peaking:
    type: integer
    label: White Peaking
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
    help: "From (WPK?): white peaking, 0 to 100. Applies to video sources only."
  warp_setting:
    type: integer
    label: Warp Setting
    min: 0
    max: 4
    step: 1
    help: "From (WAP?): the saved warp setting in use, 1 to 4, or 0 when warping is off."
  blend_setting:
    type: integer
    label: Blend Setting
    min: 0
    max: 4
    step: 1
    help: "From (BLD?): the saved blend setting in use, 1 to 4, or 0 when blending is off."
  light_source_info:
    type: string
    label: Light Source Info
    help: "Projector and laser diode hours as the projector reports them (ILI?), read while the projector is on."
  firmware:
    type: string
    label: Firmware
    help: "The firmware information the projector reports (FVI?), read on connect."
  serial_command_version:
    type: string
    label: Serial Command Version
    help: "The serial command version the projector reports (SIV?), read on connect."
  lens_type:
    type: string
    label: Lens Type
    help: "The lens type the projector reports (LET ?), read on connect."
  status_report:
    type: string
    label: Status Report
    help: "The projector's answer to Query Status (SST?)."
  last_error:
    type: string
    label: Last Error
    help: "The projector's own words for the last command it could not carry out, such as \"ITP: Too Few Parameters\"."

commands:
  # ── Power ──
  # No sets: the projector passes through warming or cooling, which can take
  # longer than a poll.
  power_on:
    label: Power On
    help: Turns the projector on from standby. It warms up before the picture shows.
    send: "(PWR 1)"
  power_off:
    label: Power Off (Standby)
    help: Turns the projector off to standby. It cools down first.
    send: "(PWR 0)"
  standby_communication_mode:
    label: Standby Mode - Communication
    help: Keeps the projector on the network while it is off, so it can be turned on and read from OpenAVC. The projector's default.
    send: "(PWR+STBM 1)"
  standby_eco_mode:
    label: Standby Mode - ECO
    help: Lowers standby power to under 0.5 W. The projector leaves the network while it is off.
    send: "(PWR+STBM 0)"
    confirm: In ECO standby the projector leaves the network when it is turned off, so OpenAVC cannot turn it back on until someone turns it on at the projector or with its remote.

  # ── Shutter ──
  shutter_open:
    label: Open Shutter
    help: Opens the shutter so the picture shows.
    send: "(SHU 0)"
    sets: { shutter: open }
  shutter_close:
    label: Close Shutter
    help: Closes the shutter, blanking the picture while the projector stays on.
    send: "(SHU 1)"
    sets: { shutter: closed }

  # ── Input ──
  # The reference's SIN table also gives 7 = 3G-SDI for two JS models; the
  # Korus has 12G-SDI (product page and the 4K row of the same table).
  select_input:
    label: Select Input
    help: Shows the chosen input.
    send: "(SIN {input})"
    params:
      input:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Input
        values:
          - { value: "3", label: "HDMI 1" }
          - { value: "4", label: "HDMI 2" }
          - { value: "6", label: "DisplayPort" }
          - { value: "7", label: "12G-SDI" }
          - { value: "8", label: "HDBaseT" }

  # ── Picture ──
  set_picture_mode:
    label: Set Picture Mode
    help: Applies a picture mode, which sets gamma, sharpness, white peaking, brightness, contrast, color and the RGB gains together.
    send: "(PST {mode})"
    params:
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Picture Mode
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Video" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Bright" }
          - { value: "2", label: "Enhanced" }
          - { value: "3", label: "REC709" }
          - { value: "4", label: "DICOM SIM" }
          - { value: "5", label: "Blending" }
          - { value: "6", label: "HDR" }
          - { value: "7", label: "3D" }
          - { value: "8", label: "High Frame Rate" }
          - { value: "9", label: "User" }
  set_brightness:
    label: Set Brightness
    help: Sets the picture brightness, 0 to 100 (50 is the default).
    send: "(BRT {level})"
    params:
      level:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Brightness
        min: 0
        max: 100
  set_contrast:
    label: Set Contrast
    help: Sets the picture contrast, 0 to 100 (50 is the default).
    send: "(CON {level})"
    params:
      level:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Contrast
        min: 0
        max: 100
  set_color:
    label: Set Color Saturation
    help: Sets the color saturation, 0 to 100 (50 is the default).
    send: "(CLR {level})"
    params:
      level:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Color
        min: 0
        max: 100
  set_sharpness:
    label: Set Sharpness
    help: Sets the edge clarity of the picture.
    send: "(DTL {level})"
    params:
      level:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Sharpness
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Maximum" }
          - { value: "1", label: "High" }
          - { value: "2", label: "Normal" }
          - { value: "3", label: "Low" }
          - { value: "4", label: "Minimum" }
  set_color_temperature:
    label: Set Color Temperature
    help: Applies a color temperature to the picture.
    send: "(CCI {temperature})"
    params:
      temperature:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Color Temperature
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Warmest" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Warm" }
          - { value: "2", label: "Cool" }
  set_gamma:
    label: Set Gamma
    help: Selects the gamma curve the picture uses.
    send: "(BGC {curve})"
    params:
      curve:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Gamma
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Video" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Film" }
          - { value: "2", label: "Bright" }
          - { value: "3", label: "Computer (CRT)" }
          - { value: "4", label: "DICOM" }
          - { value: "5", label: "HDR" }
  set_image_size:
    label: Set Image Size
    help: Sets how the picture is sized on the screen.
    send: "(SZP {size})"
    params:
      size:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Image Size
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Auto" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Native" }
          - { value: "2", label: "4:3" }
          - { value: "3", label: "Letterbox" }
          - { value: "4", label: "Full Size" }
          - { value: "5", label: "Full Width" }
          - { value: "6", label: "Full Height" }
          - { value: "7", label: "Custom" }
          - { value: "8", label: "3D Mode" }
          - { value: "9", label: "21:9" }
  freeze:
    label: Freeze Picture
    help: Freezes the picture on the current frame.
    send: "(FRZ 1)"
  unfreeze:
    label: Unfreeze Picture
    help: Lets the picture move again.
    send: "(FRZ 0)"

  # ── Test patterns ──
  # TPG+ITPG, the fuller of the reference's two test pattern commands (ITP
  # has six patterns).
  show_test_pattern:
    label: Show Test Pattern
    help: Shows a test pattern in place of the input.
    send: "(TPG+ITPG {pattern})"
    params:
      pattern:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Test Pattern
        values:
          - { value: "1", label: "Grid" }
          - { value: "2", label: "White" }
          - { value: "3", label: "Black" }
          - { value: "4", label: "Checkerboard" }
          - { value: "5", label: "Color Bars" }
          - { value: "6", label: "Red" }
          - { value: "7", label: "Green" }
          - { value: "8", label: "Blue" }
          - { value: "9", label: "Yellow" }
          - { value: "10", label: "Magenta" }
          - { value: "11", label: "Cyan" }
          - { value: "12", label: "Boresight" }
          - { value: "13", label: "Full Screen" }
          - { value: "14", label: "13 Points" }
  test_pattern_off:
    label: Test Pattern Off
    help: Takes the test pattern off and shows the input again.
    send: "(TPG+ITPG 0)"

  # ── Light source ──
  set_light_mode:
    label: Set Light Source Mode
    help: Sets how the laser light source is driven.
    send: "(LPM {mode})"
    params:
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Light Source Mode
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Constant Power" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Constant Intensity" }
          - { value: "2", label: "ECO (80% brightness)" }
          - { value: "3", label: "ECO (50% brightness)" }
  set_light_power:
    label: Set Light Power
    help: Sets the laser power used in Constant Power mode, from 1 (10%) to 100 (100%).
    send: "(LPP {power})"
    params:
      power:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Laser Power
        min: 1
        max: 100

  # ── Lens ──
  # Focus, zoom and the one-step shifts take n (next) or p (previous) only:
  # the reference gives no absolute position for any of them.
  focus_plus:
    label: Focus +
    help: Moves the lens focus one step.
    send: "(FCS n)"
  focus_minus:
    label: Focus -
    help: Moves the lens focus one step the other way.
    send: "(FCS p)"
  zoom_in:
    label: Zoom +
    help: Zooms the lens one step.
    send: "(ZOM n)"
  zoom_out:
    label: Zoom -
    help: Zooms the lens one step the other way.
    send: "(ZOM p)"
  lens_shift_up:
    label: Lens Shift Up
    help: Moves the picture up by the number of steps given.
    send: "(LVU {steps})"
    params:
      steps:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Steps
        default: 10
        min: 0
        max: 100
  lens_shift_down:
    label: Lens Shift Down
    help: Moves the picture down by the number of steps given.
    send: "(LVD {steps})"
    params:
      steps:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Steps
        default: 10
        min: 0
        max: 100
  lens_shift_left:
    label: Lens Shift Left
    help: Moves the picture left by the number of steps given.
    send: "(LHL {steps})"
    params:
      steps:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Steps
        default: 10
        min: 0
        max: 100
  lens_shift_right:
    label: Lens Shift Right
    help: Moves the picture right by the number of steps given.
    send: "(LHR {steps})"
    params:
      steps:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Steps
        default: 10
        min: 0
        max: 100
  lens_center:
    label: Center Lens
    help: Moves the lens to its center position. Zoom and focus stay where they are.
    send: "(LCB+HOME 1)"
  lens_lock:
    label: Lock Lens
    help: Locks the zoom, focus and shift motors so the lens cannot be moved by accident.
    send: "(LCB+LOCK 1)"
  lens_unlock:
    label: Unlock Lens
    help: Lets the zoom, focus and shift motors move again.
    send: "(LCB+LOCK 0)"
  recall_lens_memory:
    label: Recall Lens Memory
    help: Moves the lens to a saved lens memory position.
    send: "(LMA {memory})"
    params:
      memory:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Lens Memory
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Memory 1" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Memory 2" }
          - { value: "2", label: "Memory 3" }
          - { value: "3", label: "Memory 4" }
          - { value: "4", label: "Memory 5" }
  save_lens_memory:
    label: Save Lens Memory
    help: Saves the current lens position to a lens memory, replacing what was saved there.
    send: "(LMS {memory})"
    params:
      memory:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Lens Memory
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Memory 1" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Memory 2" }
          - { value: "2", label: "Memory 3" }
          - { value: "3", label: "Memory 4" }
          - { value: "4", label: "Memory 5" }

  # ── On-screen display and keys ──
  osd_show:
    label: Show On-Screen Display
    help: Lets the projector show its menus and messages on screen.
    send: "(OSD 1)"
    sets: { osd: true }
  osd_hide:
    label: Hide On-Screen Display
    help: Hides the projector's menus and messages from the screen.
    send: "(OSD 0)"
    sets: { osd: false }
  send_key:
    label: Press Remote Key
    help: Presses a key on the projector's remote, for working the on-screen menu from OpenAVC.
    send: "(KEY {key})"
    params:
      key:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Key
        values:
          - { value: "19", label: "Menu" }
          - { value: "20", label: "Exit" }
          - { value: "38", label: "Up" }
          - { value: "42", label: "Down" }
          - { value: "39", label: "Left" }
          - { value: "41", label: "Right" }
          - { value: "40", label: "Enter" }
          - { value: "48", label: "Input" }
          - { value: "47", label: "Auto" }
          - { value: "66", label: "Info" }
          - { value: "49", label: "OSD" }
          - { value: "35", label: "Help" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Test" }

  # ── Queries ──
  query_power:
    label: Query Power
    help: Reads the power state.
    send: "(PWR?)"
  query_shutter:
    label: Query Shutter
    help: Reads whether the shutter is open.
    send: "(SHU?)"
  query_active_source:
    label: Query Active Source
    help: Reads the active source and its resolution.
    send: "(MIF+ACTS?)(MIF+RESL?)"
  query_light_source:
    label: Query Light Source Info
    help: Reads the projector and laser diode hours.
    send: "(ILI?)"
  query_firmware:
    label: Query Firmware
    help: Reads the firmware information and the serial command version.
    send: "(FVI?)(SIV?)"
  query_lens_type:
    label: Query Lens Type
    help: Reads the lens type.
    send: "(LET ?)"
  query_status:
    label: Query Status
    help: Reads the projector's status report into Status Report.
    send: "(SST?)"

  # ── Raw escape hatch ──
  send_command:
    label: Send Raw Command
    help: Sends a command from the Korus Series Serial Commands reference exactly as typed, parentheses included, such as (HDR+HDRE 1).
    send: "{raw}"
    params:
      raw:
        type: string
        required: true
        label: Raw Command
        description: The full message including its parentheses, e.g. "(PWR 1)" or "(MIF+RESL?)".

# Every rule allows a line ending before the reply (left over from the
# previous one), leading zeros, and the number-plus-quoted-label form the
# reference's format table shows. The closing parenthesis is the frame end,
# so it is not part of the text a rule sees.
responses:
  - match: '\(PWR!\s*0*(\d+)'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: power, map: { "0": "off", "1": "on", "10": cooling, "11": warming } }
  - match: '\(SHU!\s*0*([01])\b'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: shutter, map: { "0": open, "1": closed } }
  - match: '\(OSD!\s*0*([01])\b'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: osd, map: { "0": false, "1": true } }
  - match: '\(WPK!\s*0*(\d+)'
    set: { white_peaking: "$1" }
  - match: '\(WAP!\s*0*(\d+)'
    set: { warp_setting: "$1" }
  - match: '\(BLD!\s*0*(\d+)'
    set: { blend_setting: "$1" }
  # The active source: a quoted name when there is one, else the value.
  - match: '\(MIF\+ACTS!\s*(?:\d+\s+)?"?([^"]+?)"?\s*$'
    set: { active_source: "$1" }
  # The read-only information replies: one quoted string has its quotes
  # taken off; anything else is kept as the projector sent it.
  - match: '\(MIF\+RESL!\s*"([^"]*)"\s*$'
    set: { signal_resolution: "$1" }
  - match: '\(MIF\+RESL!\s*(.*?)\s*$'
    set: { signal_resolution: "$1" }
  - match: '\(ILI!\s*"([^"]*)"\s*$'
    set: { light_source_info: "$1" }
  - match: '\(ILI!\s*(.*?)\s*$'
    set: { light_source_info: "$1" }
  - match: '\(FVI!\s*"([^"]*)"\s*$'
    set: { firmware: "$1" }
  - match: '\(FVI!\s*(.*?)\s*$'
    set: { firmware: "$1" }
  - match: '\(SIV!\s*"([^"]*)"\s*$'
    set: { serial_command_version: "$1" }
  - match: '\(SIV!\s*(.*?)\s*$'
    set: { serial_command_version: "$1" }
  - match: '\(LET!\s*"([^"]*)"\s*$'
    set: { lens_type: "$1" }
  - match: '\(LET!\s*(.*?)\s*$'
    set: { lens_type: "$1" }
  - match: '\(SST!\s*"([^"]*)"\s*$'
    set: { status_report: "$1" }
  - match: '\(SST!\s*(.*?)\s*$'
    set: { status_report: "$1" }
  # (65535 00000 ERR00005 "ITP: Too Few Parameters")
  - match: '\(\d+\s+\d+\s+ERR\d+\s+"([^"]*)"'
    set: { last_error: "$1" }

device_settings:
  osd_enabled:
    type: boolean
    label: On-Screen Display
    help: Shows (on) or hides (off) the projector's menus and messages on screen (OSD).
    state_key: osd
    default: true
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "(OSD {value:d})"
  white_peaking:
    type: integer
    min: 0
    max: 100
    label: White Peaking
    help: Raises the brightness of whites, 0 to 100 (WPK). Applies to video sources only.
    state_key: white_peaking
    default: 100
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "(WPK {value})"
  warp_setting:
    type: integer
    min: 0
    max: 4
    label: Warp Setting
    help: Applies a saved warp setting, 1 to 4, or 0 to turn warping off (WAP).
    state_key: warp_setting
    default: 0
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "(WAP {value})"
  blend_setting:
    type: integer
    min: 0
    max: 4
    label: Blend Setting
    help: Applies a saved blend setting, 1 to 4, or 0 to turn blending off (BLD).
    state_key: blend_setting
    default: 0
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "(BLD {value})"

quick_actions: [power_on, power_off, shutter_open, shutter_close]

on_connect:
  - { send: "(FVI?)", query_for: firmware }
  - { send: "(SIV?)", query_for: serial_command_version }
  - { send: "(LET ?)", query_for: lens_type }

# Power and shutter in every state; the rest only while the projector is on,
# when there is a source and a picture to read.
polling:
  queries:
    - { send: "(PWR?)", query_for: power }
    - { send: "(SHU?)", query_for: shutter }
    - { send: "(MIF+ACTS?)", query_for: active_source, when_state: { power: "on" } }
    - { send: "(MIF+RESL?)", query_for: signal_resolution, when_state: { power: "on" } }
    - { send: "(OSD?)", query_for: osd, when_state: { power: "on" } }
    - { send: "(WPK?)", query_for: white_peaking, when_state: { power: "on" } }
    - { send: "(WAP?)", query_for: warp_setting, when_state: { power: "on" } }
    - { send: "(BLD?)", query_for: blend_setting, when_state: { power: "on" } }
    - { send: "(ILI?)", query_for: light_source_info, when_state: { power: "on" } }

# Over a direct RS-232 line nothing else notices a projector that stops
# answering. (PWR?) answers in Communication-mode standby; any reply counts,
# an error included.
liveness:
  send: "(PWR?)"
  expect: '\((?:PWR!|\d+\s+\d+\s+ERR)'
  interval: 30
  timeout: 5
  max_failures: 2

# ── Simulator ──
# A projector in Communication-mode standby. Requests are answered in the
# forms the reference shows: (PWR!001 "On") with a label, (SHU!0) bare, and
# the documented error for a code sent with no value. A set draws no reply
# (the reference prints none), so a change shows at the next poll. Power
# goes straight to on or off: the simulator does not model warming or
# cooling. The read-only replies (MIF, FVI, SIV, LET, ILI, SST) are
# placeholders: the reference prints none of them. An unknown request draws
# no reply.
simulator:
  initial_state:
    power: "off"
    shutter: closed
    input: "3"
    osd: true
    white_peaking: 100
    warp_setting: 0
    blend_setting: 0
    brightness: 50
    contrast: 50
    picture_mode: "1"
    light_mode: "0"
    test_pattern: "0"
    freeze: false
    signal_resolution: "3840x2160 60Hz"
    firmware: "1.1.0"
    serial_command_version: "1.0"
    lens_type: "Standard Zoom Lens"
    light_source_info: "1234 1200"
  controls:
    - type: select
      key: power
      label: Power
      options: ["off", "on", warming, cooling]
    - type: select
      key: shutter
      label: Shutter
      options: [open, closed]
    - type: select
      key: input
      label: Input
      options: ["3", "4", "6", "7", "8"]
    - type: toggle
      key: osd
      label: On-Screen Display
    - type: slider
      key: white_peaking
      label: White Peaking
      min: 0
      max: 100
    - type: slider
      key: warp_setting
      label: Warp Setting
      min: 0
      max: 4
    - type: slider
      key: blend_setting
      label: Blend Setting
      min: 0
      max: 4
  command_handlers:
    # One dispatcher for the whole grammar. Several messages can arrive in
    # one read (a poll goes out 50 ms apart, with no line ending), so every
    # message in the read is handled and the replies go back together.
    - match: '(?s:\s*(?:\([^()]*\)\s*)+)'
      handler: |
        def _run():
            POWER_CODES = {"off": ("000", "Standby"), "on": ("001", "On"),
                           "cooling": ("010", "Cooling"), "warming": ("011", "Warm Up")}
            SOURCES = {"3": "HDMI1", "4": "HDMI2", "6": "DisplayPort",
                       "7": "12G-SDI", "8": "HDBaseT"}
            out = []
            for msg in re.findall(r"\(([^()]*)\)", match.group(0)):
                m = re.match(r"^\s*[#$&]?([A-Za-z]{3})(?:\+([A-Za-z0-9#]{4}))?\s*(\?)?\s*(.*?)\s*$", msg)
                if not m:
                    continue
                code = m.group(1).upper()
                sub = (m.group(2) or "").upper()
                ask = m.group(3) == "?"
                value = m.group(4)
                key = code + ("+" + sub if sub else "")
                if ask:
                    if key == "PWR":
                        num, label = POWER_CODES.get(str(state["power"]), ("000", "Standby"))
                        out.append("(PWR!" + num + ' "' + label + '")')
                    elif key == "SHU":
                        out.append("(SHU!" + ("0" if state["shutter"] == "open" else "1") + ")")
                    elif key == "OSD":
                        out.append("(OSD!" + str(int(bool(state["osd"]))) + ")")
                    elif key == "WPK":
                        out.append("(WPK!" + str(int(state["white_peaking"])) + ")")
                    elif key == "WAP":
                        out.append("(WAP!" + str(int(state["warp_setting"])) + ")")
                    elif key == "BLD":
                        out.append("(BLD!" + str(int(state["blend_setting"])) + ")")
                    elif key == "MIF+ACTS":
                        src = str(state["input"])
                        out.append("(MIF+ACTS!" + src.zfill(3) + ' "' + SOURCES.get(src, src) + '")')
                    elif key == "MIF+RESL":
                        out.append('(MIF+RESL!"' + str(state["signal_resolution"]) + '")')
                    elif key == "FVI":
                        out.append('(FVI!"' + str(state["firmware"]) + '")')
                    elif key == "SIV":
                        out.append('(SIV!"' + str(state["serial_command_version"]) + '")')
                    elif key == "LET":
                        out.append('(LET!"' + str(state["lens_type"]) + '")')
                    elif key == "ILI":
                        out.append('(ILI!"' + str(state["light_source_info"]) + '")')
                    elif key == "SST":
                        out.append('(SST!"Normal")')
                    continue
                if value == "":
                    # The reference's own example: (ITP) answers this.
                    out.append('(65535 00000 ERR00005 "' + code + ': Too Few Parameters")')
                    continue
                if key == "PWR" and value in ("0", "1"):
                    state["power"] = "on" if value == "1" else "off"
                elif key == "SHU" and value in ("0", "1"):
                    state["shutter"] = "open" if value == "0" else "closed"
                elif key == "SIN" or key == "SIN+MAIN":
                    if value in SOURCES:
                        state["input"] = value
                elif key == "OSD" and value in ("0", "1"):
                    state["osd"] = value == "1"
                elif key == "WPK" and value.isdigit() and int(value) <= 100:
                    state["white_peaking"] = int(value)
                elif key == "WAP" and value.isdigit() and int(value) <= 4:
                    state["warp_setting"] = int(value)
                elif key == "BLD" and value.isdigit() and int(value) <= 4:
                    state["blend_setting"] = int(value)
                elif key == "BRT" and value.isdigit():
                    state["brightness"] = int(value)
                elif key == "CON" and value.isdigit():
                    state["contrast"] = int(value)
                elif key == "PST":
                    state["picture_mode"] = value
                elif key == "LPM":
                    state["light_mode"] = value
                elif key == "TPG+ITPG":
                    state["test_pattern"] = value
                elif key == "FRZ" and value in ("0", "1"):
                    state["freeze"] = value == "1"
            if out:
                respond("".join(out))
        _run()
